course: dictionary
lesson: 13854
title: Journalistic writing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Journalism is the work of collecting and writing the news for newspapers, websites, radio and TV. A journalist finds out what has happened and tells people about it. When we say something is journalistic, we mean it is to do with that work, or written in its style.
explain: Journalistic writing is clear, short and based on facts. «The council voted to build a new bridge on Mill Road at noon on Monday.» This sentence tells you who, what, when and where, and it does not add the writer's opinion. That is the journalistic style.
explain: Journalists follow some simple rules. They check facts before they print them, and they hear both sides of a story. Citizenship lessons teach why this matters: a fair report helps people make up their own minds.
fact: Journalism means the work of collecting and writing the news.
fact: Journalistic means to do with journalism, or written in the style of news.
fact: «Journalistic» writing is clear, short and based on facts.
fact: A journalist checks facts and hears both sides of a story before writing it.
fact: A news report often answers who, what, when, where and why.
exercise: «The report gave the facts: who, what, when and where, with no opinions.» What kind of writing is this? | *Journalistic | Poetic | Silly | Secret
exercise: Maya says a good news report should check facts and hear both sides. Which journalistic rule is she describing? | Writing with lots of adjectives | Printing the story first and checking later | Using only the writer's own opinion | *Checking facts and hearing both sides
exercise: A report says: «Fire crews rescued a cat from a tree on Oak Road at 9 a.m. on Monday.» Which question does it answer best? | How the writer felt about the cat | *When and where it happened | What the writer had for lunch | Whether cats are nice
test: Which word describes writing in the style of news: clear, short and based on facts? | Musical | Comical | Dramatic | *Journalistic
test: Why do journalists check facts before printing a story? | So the story is longer | *To make sure the news is true | Because it is quicker to print | Because it makes the newspaper thicker
exam: Which sentence is most journalistic? | *Yesterday, the council voted to build a new bridge on Mill Road at noon. | Once upon a time there was a bridge. | Oh, what a wonderful, magical morning! | I think the bridge is probably a nice idea.
